          {
            "type": "reasoning",
            "text": "The message was delivered to tea-reader. The tea-reader subordinate is working. Its report will arrive as an event that wakes me. I should end my turn now and wait for the tea-reader's report. The user asked me to collect its answer and tell them what it said — I can't do that until the report arrives.\n\nI've done:\n1. Hired tea-reader with mission to read delegate-note.txt and report exact contents. ✓\n2. Sent it a message asking it to keep its answer to one sentence. ✓ (delivered, starts_now)\n\nNow I need to wait for its answer. The subordinate's report arrives as an event that wakes me. So I should end my turn here and let the user know I'm waiting for tea-reader's report... Actually, should I end the turn with a brief note? The instructions say: \"A busy agent is never blocked on — your message is queued immediately for its own mode-homogeneous turn, so send follow-ups as soon as you have them.\" And \"subordinate reports and peer replies then arrive as events that wake you.\"\n\nSo yes, I end my turn, and when tea-reader reports, I'll be woken and can relay the answer to the user.\n\nLet me end with a concise status: hired tea-reader, sent the one-sentence request, waiting for its report; I'll relay what it says when it arrives."
          },
